# Rank 1223 / CVE-2018-14721: jackson-databind's BeanDeserializerFactory
# (src/main/java/com/fasterxml/jackson/databind/deser/BeanDeserializerFactory.java)
# keeps a hardcoded denylist of classes its own deserializer refuses to
# instantiate through polymorphic/default typing, DEFAULT_NO_DESER_CLASS_NAMES,
# checked by checkIllegalTypes via the instance field it seeds once,
# _cfgIllegalClassNames (CWE-184: incomplete blacklist). Fix
# 87d29af25e82a249ea15858e2d4ecbf64091db44 (`git show`, VERSION and
# BeanDeserializerFactory.java only) adds four class names research had shown
# reachable through polymorphic deserialization gadget chains --
# org.slf4j.ext.EventData, flex.messaging.util.concurrent.
# AsynchBeansWorkManagerExecutor, com.sun.deploy.security.ruleset.DRSHelper,
# org.apache.axis2.jaxws.spi.handler.HandlerResolverImpl -- the last of which
# triggers an outbound WSDL fetch on instantiation, which is what earns this
# CVE its CWE-918 (SSRF) tag alongside the advisory's CWE-502 lineage. No
# other file changes.
#
# No flag on the pool row. Verified the commit is the real, targeted fix per
# Step 0's note that flags are not the only source of pool errors: the diff
# is exactly a release-notes line plus four `s.add(...)` calls appended to
# the same static initializer the CVE's advisory names, nothing else.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap. `vyql match` on the real file (both the pre-fix and
# post-fix revision) labels no source or sink anywhere near the denylist or
# checkIllegalTypes; the only evidence at all is
# `_cfgIllegalClassNames.contains -> vyql.internal.NeutralizerAssumption`, a
# generic control marker that says "this call looks like a containment
# check", not anything that judges whether the checked set is complete.
#
# The shape needed to catch this already exists and already ships: a
# class-scope presence binding keying on the class name, an identifier a
# method in that class references, and the literal set the class's own
# denylist field holds -- exactly what code.H2oJdbcDisallowedParameterDenylistGap
# / VYQL-INJ-205 (rank 1156, vyql/bindings/java/m420f6b67.vyql binding
# h2oJdbcDisallowedParameterDenylistGap458) already does for H2O's
# SQLManager. Building the identical binding for BeanDeserializerFactory,
# class name "BeanDeserializerFactory", identifier "_cfgIllegalClassNames"
# (the field checkIllegalTypes actually reads -- no method in this class
# refers to DEFAULT_NO_DESER_CLASS_NAMES by its own name), and the same
# missing-literal-set shape, fires correctly on a trimmed repro that mirrors
# the real class's structure. It does not fire on the real file. Instrumenting
# lowering.go's classContextAnalysisEvent directly and scanning the real,
# unmodified BeanDeserializerFactory.java (898 lines) shows why:
# memberTokens for this class hits exactly 512 entries -- the hardcoded cap
# in classMemberContextTokens (internal/extract/lowering/lowering.go:1330,
# `if len(tokens) > 512 { return tokens[:512] }`) and the same limit in
# classContextAnalysisEvent's own `add` closure (lowering.go:1267,
# `len(tokens) >= 512`) -- before the walk ever reaches checkIllegalTypes,
# one of the last methods declared in this ~40-method class. The token
# `identifier:_cfgIllegalClassNames` that the binding's condition needs is
# never added to the set the query engine evaluates against, so no vocabulary
# or binding, however precisely aimed, can see it. (No definition change
# ships with this rank -- the binding above was built and tested to
# characterize the gap, then reverted.)
#
# Isolated CVE-independently, using the already-shipped VYQL-INJ-205
# pipeline and no jackson-databind vocabulary at all: H2O's own SQLManager,
# padded with unrelated methods ahead of validateJdbcUrl (same
# DEFAULT_JDBC_DISALLOWED_PARAMETERS field and check, untouched), stops
# firing once enough padding methods precede it. 3 padding methods still
# fires; 14 does not -- both below, as the test pair. This confirms the cap
# is a general per-class token budget, not anything about jackson's own
# class shape, this CVE's vocabulary, or a binding that does not yet exist.
#
# Scanning the real checkout (bounded, timeout 180, --max-ram 4GB, --cache
# off) confirms the same absence at scale: both revisions report 46
# findings (36 at or above high), an identical set of rule IDs on both, and
# neither output names BeanDeserializerFactory.java anywhere.
#
# Raising or removing the 512-token cap is a small diff -- two constants,
# one file -- but not a bounded, risk-free one: an unbounded (or much
# larger) per-class token budget changes what every other class-scope
# binding already shipped sees on every large real class in the corpus, not
# just this one, and needs to be weighed against scan cost and precision
# across the whole benchmark before it ships, not bumped in isolation inside
# this rank's review. Recorded here rather than attempted.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, isolating pair below.

test "rank1223: H2O's SQLManager denylist check, with 3 unrelated methods ahead of it, is still caught"
  lang java
  file h2o-core/src/main/java/water/jdbc/SQLManager.java
  expect VYQL-INJ-205
  code
  ```
  package water.jdbc;

  import java.util.List;
  import java.util.stream.Collectors;
  import java.util.stream.Stream;

  public class SQLManager {

    private static final List<String> DEFAULT_JDBC_DISALLOWED_PARAMETERS = Stream.of(
            "autoDeserialize", "queryInterceptors", "allowLoadLocalInfile", "allowMultiQueries",
            "allowLoadLocalInfileInPath", "allowUrlInLocalInfile", "allowPublicKeyRetrieval",
            "init", "script", "shutdown"
    ).map(String::toLowerCase).collect(Collectors.toList());

    public void paddingMethod0(String argOne, String argTwo, String argThree) {
        System.out.println("padding method number 0 does some unrelated logging work here");
        String local0 = argOne + argTwo + argThree;
        if (local0.length() > 3) {
            System.out.println("branch taken in padding method 0 for extra token volume");
        }
    }
    public void paddingMethod1(String argOne, String argTwo, String argThree) {
        System.out.println("padding method number 1 does some unrelated logging work here");
        String local1 = argOne + argTwo + argThree;
        if (local1.length() > 3) {
            System.out.println("branch taken in padding method 1 for extra token volume");
        }
    }
    public void paddingMethod2(String argOne, String argTwo, String argThree) {
        System.out.println("padding method number 2 does some unrelated logging work here");
        String local2 = argOne + argTwo + argThree;
        if (local2.length() > 3) {
            System.out.println("branch taken in padding method 2 for extra token volume");
        }
    }

    public static void validateJdbcUrl(String jdbcUrl) {
        for (String key : parseParameterNames(jdbcUrl)) {
            if (DEFAULT_JDBC_DISALLOWED_PARAMETERS.contains(key.toLowerCase())) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Potentially dangerous JDBC parameter found: " + key);
            }
        }
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1223: the identical denylist check, with 14 unrelated methods ahead of it, is invisible"
